  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1...Upslope snow event through Saturday for the \nwestern mountains, much colder air through the weekend before a \nwarmup by late next week. \n\nEmbedded shortwave disturbances rounding the axis of the\nlonger-wave trough currently over the eastern Conus look to\nenhance upslope snow and rain showers across the area through \nSaturday. These upslope snow showers will only effect the higher\nterrain locations across the western mountains of NC/VA/WV.\nGiven that the ground temperatures across the area are still\nfairly warm given the recent above average temperatures across\nthe region, any snow that does fall will likely melt. Any\naccumulations that do occur will likely be across the highest\nterrain areas. Flurries will be possible anywhere along/west of \nI-81 today, and generally up to 0.5\" is possible for the \nwestern mountains outside of the higher elevations, where 1-3\" \nis possible, especially on elevated or grassy surfaces. It is \npossible a few areas such as Western Greenbrier County or Mt. \nRogers could exceed 3\", but confidence in that occurring is low.\nSnow showers look to continue through midday Saturday before\ndiminishing as the aforementioned upper level trough lifts off \nto the northeast. \n\nTemperatures across the area will be much cooler compared to\nearlier in the month, as the upper level trough remains over\nthe region through the first half of the weekend. These lower\n500mb heights combined with surface high pressure settling\ndirectly over the region by Monday night will lead to several\nnights of ideal radiational cooling with clear skies and calm\nwinds. These 500mb heights combined with 850mb temperatures in\nthe -5 to -10 degree Celsius range will keep high temperatures\n5-10 degrees below normal for the Piedmont, and 15-20 degrees\nbelow normal for locations west of the Blue Ridge. Highs each\nday through the weekend will remain in the upper 40s to low 50s\nin the Piedmont, with 30s and 40s for mountain locations.\nOvernight lows look to fall into the 20s areawide both Saturday\nand Sunday nights. \n\nWinds will also remain elevated through the weekend, gusting to\n20-25 mph today and Saturday, lowering to around 20 mph on \nSunday and becoming light by Monday as high pressure builds back\ninto the area. Due to these winds along with the cold \ntemperatures, wind chills will be in the teens/20s Saturday and \nSunday mornings. While this is not a dangerous level of cold, it\nwill be quite the shock to the system after the mild last week \nor so. By early next week, a gradual warming trend occurs, with \nhighs in the 50s by Tuesday and in the 60s by Thursday with no \nrain expected all week.",
